The scaling itself isn't the real problem — it's that the hitbox that receives pointer events grows with the visual, since CardHover scales the same RectTransform that Unity's raycaster uses for hit-testing. When it scales up, its footprint now overlaps neighboring cards, so their raycast target gets covered and they never get OnPointerEnter.

The fix is to separate "what gets hit-tested" from "what gets scaled visually":

In the Card prefab, create an empty child object called Visual and move all the visible parts (card image, edge image, labels) under it.
Make sure only the root object's graphic (or an invisible full-size Image on the root) has Raycast Target = true. Turn Raycast Target off on all the Images inside Visual. That way the clickable/hoverable area is always the root's fixed-size rect — the enlarged visual just renders on top without intercepting the pointer.
CardHover then scales/lifts Visual, not the root RectTransform.

